{
  "dom_id": "question-134041",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Mark drank $\\frac{1}{2}$ of the amount of orange juice that he bought. What **exact** amount of juice did Mark drink?\n\n*Remember that Mark bought $\\frac{7}{4}$ liters of orange juice.*\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Mark drank\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \nliters of orange juice.",
  "worked_solution": "We know that Mark has $\\frac{7}{4}$ of a liter of orange juice.\n\nTo find the fraction of a liter that Mark drank, we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{2}$ by $\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nTo multiply two fractions, we multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{4}=\\frac{1\\times 7}{2\\times 4}=\\frac{7}{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike drank $\\frac{7}{8}$ liters of orange juice.",
